<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="hydroxyjasmonate sulfate biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-6235" dataSource="PlantCyc" />
  <Description>Jasmonates are an important class of compounds, related to |FRAME:Jasmonic-Acids &quot;jasmonic acid&quot;|, found in many, if not all, plant species. These compounds may participate in a number of biological processes including resistance to herbivores and some pathogens, senescence, reproductive development, and protection against ozone (reviewed in |CITS:[19025383][16807821][17513307]|). Many different jasmonate precursors and jasmonate derivatives, including |FRAME:CPD-11253| (12-OHJA) and |FRAME: 12-Hydroxyjasmonate-Sulfates| (12-OHJA sulfate) have been identified throughout the plant kingdom |CITS:[ 17995915]|. No biological activity has been attributed to the sulfated 12-OHJA derivative. Unlike the active compound, methyl JA, |FRAME: 12-Hydroxyjasmonate-Sulfates| does not inhibit germination or root elongation in tomato seedlings |CITS: [17995915]|. Moreover, this is not likely to be a hydrolyzable storage form of hydroxyjasmonate because the Arabidopsis genome is not predicted to encode sulfatases |CITS:[12637544]|. The biological relevance of 12-OHJA is more ambiguous. |FRAME: CPD-11253| behaves the same as |FRAME: 12-Hydroxyjasmonate-Sulfates| in root growth and germination assays in tomato, suggesting that it is also biologically inactive in that species |CITS:[17995915]|. However, 12-OHJA can induce tuber formation in potatoes |CITS:[HELDER93]|. 12-OHJA also prompts distinct sets of transcriptional changes when compared to other jasmonates in tomato |CITS:[17995915]|. Neither of these compounds has been functionally characterized in &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis thaliana&lt;/i&gt;. Two enzymes are required for this biological pathway. To date, no hydroxylase capable of catalyzing the formation of |FRAME:CPD-11253| or |FRAME: 11-Hydroxyjasmonates| have been identified in plants. But, in vitro analyses have led to the indentification of an enzyme, |FRAME:AT5G07010-MONOMER|, capable of acting on both these substrates |CITS:[12637544]|. As seen with other aspects of the JA biosynthetic pathway, there may be multiple levels of regulation controlling the synthesis of these compounds. For example, transcript levels of the ST2A enzyme are up-regulated by its substrate, |FRAME:CPD-11253| indicating a possible feed-forward loop to tightly regulate the level of free 12-OHJA in &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis thaliana&lt;/i&gt; |CITS:[12637544]|.</Description>
